    <exercise workspace="6in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Coepods aare a low-level consumer in estuarian foodwebs. In G. Weiss's paper <pubtitle>Development and Lipid Composition of the Harpacticoid Cpepod Nitocra Spinipes Reared on Different Diets</pubtitle>, a random sample of copepods were grown in different containers. One group was fed lipid-rich diatoms, another bacteria, and the third was fed leafy macroalgae. There were 12 containers total, four replicates for each diet. Each container was seeded with five gravid (egg-bearing) females and the number of copepods in each container was measured 14 days later. The data is summarized in the table below. 
            </p>
            <tabular>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell>Diet</cell> <cell>R1</cell> <cell>R2</cell> <cell>R3</cell> <cell>R4</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Diatoms</cell> <cell>426</cell> <cell>467</cell> <cell>438</cell> <cell>497</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Bacteria</cell> <cell>303</cell> <cell>301</cell> <cell>293</cell> <cell>328</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Macroalgae</cell> <cell>277</cell> <cell>324</cell> <cell>302</cell> <cell>272</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
            <p>
                Conduct a One-Way ANoVa test with 95% confidence to determine whether the copepods' diet is a contributing factor to their population. Clearly state your null and alternative hypotheses. Interpret your results in the context of the experiment with plain language. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>


</worksheet>
